<!--
    An inline system call (`sc`) lifts to a CALLOTHER with no register inputs
    and no output, so the r3 a function sets up for it still holds that value
    afterwards. `pair` returns the two argument registers it set up as a register pair, and
    `after` doubles its argument: it reads back the r3 it saved, not the result.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 flow-classification.
    P0 assertion:      option syscallregs off|auto|on  (default auto).

    Source (clang -O2 -target powerpc-linux-gnu -c, the .text bytes verbatim below):

        long pair(long a, long b) {
          register long r0 asm("r0") = 4;
          register long r3 asm("r3") = b; register long r4 asm("r4") = a;
          asm volatile("sc" : "+r"(r3) : "r"(r4), "r"(r0) : "memory", "cr0");
          return r3;
        }
        long after(long x) {
          register long r0 asm("r0") = 20; register long r3 asm("r3");
          asm volatile("sc" : "=r"(r3), "+r"(r0) :: "memory", "cr0", "r4", "r5",
                       "r6", "r7", "r8", "r9", "r10", "r11", "r12", "ctr");
          if (r3 < 0) return -1;
          return r3 + x;
        }
        long fence(long a) { asm volatile("sync" ::: "memory"); return a; }

    Pass 1 (option syscallregs on) gives the sc its effects: it writes r3,
    and it reads the number register r0 and then the argument registers in
    order, up to the first one the function did not write on every path to
    it.  Pass 2 (option syscallregs off) is the old model, and so is pass 3,
    the default `auto`: it acts only on an image the loader identified as a
    program for an operating system's user space, which a bare byte chunk is
    not.  fence is the control: its barrier is a CALLOTHER too,
    and nothing about it changes.
-->
